<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kholop">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kholop</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as kabala people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A kholop was an unfree dependant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> and later Muscovy; in the 11th and 12th centuries the term covered several categories of dependent people, especially slaves. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russkaya-pravda">Russkaya Pravda</a> set out their status: a master could kill or sell his kholop but answered for his acts. People became kholops through capture, selling themselves, debt, crime or marriage to a kholop. In 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> their status was essentially that of slaves; some served as armed retainers of landowners, and many fell into debt bondage as kabala kholops. Those who were house serfs became liable to the poll tax in 1722–1724 and were thereafter treated as ordinary serfs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kholop.jpg" alt="Kholop" width="330" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Viktor Vasnetsov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Бой_скифов_со_славянами.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word is first mentioned in a chronicle under the year 986. It is related to Slavic words for a man or a boy, such as the Ukrainian khlopets, the Polish chłopiec and the Slovak chlap, a synonym for man. A shift between the meanings young person and servant, in either direction, is common, as in the English use of boy for a domestic servant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> the status and kinds of kholops were set out in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russkaya-pravda">Russkaya Pravda</a>, a legal code of the late Kievan period. Besides killing or selling his kholop, a master could hand him over to settle a debt, and he was answerable if the kholop insulted a freeman or stole. Until the late 15th century kholops made up the majority of the servants working lordly lands. Some, mainly house serfs, joined the ranks of the princely servants, including those in military service, or took up trades, farming or administrative work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Through the 16th century the place of kholops in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> economy of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> shrank as landowners relied more on the labour of peasants. At the turn of that century service-class kholops appeared and spread across the country. The boevye kholopy, or combat kholops, formed the armed retinues and bodyguards of large and medium landowners from the 16th to the 18th century and served alongside noblemen as a considerable part of the landed army, equipped as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a>, usually in cheap quilted armour and caps. In the late 17th century there were also kholops settled on the land, who ran their own households and paid a due called chynsh, similar to quitrent.</p><p>The kabala people of the 15th to 17th centuries fell into heavy debt bondage after taking a loan and served their creditor until it was repaid. Their status was governed by the general laws on kholops, the Sudebnik of 1550 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobornoe-ulozhenie">Sobornoye Ulozheniye</a> of 1649. A decree of 1 February 1597 bound such a kholop to serve until the creditor's death, and in time all kinds of kholop were placed in the kabala category; the agreements were written in kabala books.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kholop” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Kholops</b> were the unfree of Rus’: in the 11th and 12th centuries the word covered several kinds of dependent people, and especially slaves.</blockquote><a href="/societies/kievan-rus">Social Hierarchy of Kievan Rus'</a></li></ul></section>
